{:edb/db-before-t 1884 :edb/db-after-t 1885 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535197 50 #inst "2026-10-08T19:18:38.799Z" 13194139535197 true] [17592186119611 1100 "Calculate the value of\n$\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of $a_{31}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4 \\times 4$ determinant to a $3 \\times 3$ determinant.*" 13194139535197 false] [17592186119611 1100 "Calculate the value of \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058571/assets/9cb26049b5e3d06f9ef9e07ce617042166330b1eddb84063d7970799ebf6d8e5.png)\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of $a_{31}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4 \\times 4$ determinant to a $3 \\times 3$ determinant.*" 13194139535197 true] [17592186119611 1145 "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the right of $a_{31}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (or column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\mid C_{2}:=C_{2} + 2C_{1}; C_{3}:=C_{3} + 3C_{1}; C_{4}:=C_{4} + (-3)C_{1}; =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd row, we get\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; = 1 \\cdot (-1)^{3 + 1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; = 4 \\cdot (-1)^{2 + 2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; = 4(11 - 3); = 32\n$$" 13194139535197 false] [17592186119611 1145 "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the right of $a_{31}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (or column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+3{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+(-3){C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -5 & 11\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -5 & 11\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 11\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 11\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(11-3) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535197 true] [17592186119614 1100 "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & -4\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Find a $3\\times 3$ determinant that has the same value.\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below $a_{11}$ equal to zero.*" 13194139535197 true] [17592186119614 1100 "$\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Find a $3\\times 3$ determinant that has the same value.\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below $a_{11}$ equal to zero.*" 13194139535197 false] [17592186119614 1145 "Let's make entries below $a_{11}=1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.